This is a distribution of the CDL2 Lab Redux for Windows and Linux. A single zip file is provided for both.

CONTENT
  _README                        This file.
  _LICENSE                       The statement of applicable license.
  cc.cmd                         Windows command file to compile the generated C code with debug symbols.
  cco.cmd                        Windows command file to compile the generated C code with optimizations.
  CDL2.h                         Standard header file for C code generated by the Lab. May be modified with your own addtions as necessary.
  CDL2Trace.h                    The CDL2 debugger for C. CDL2.h will inludde this file when appropriate settings are used.
  CDL2v1.lab.gz                  A Lab DB containing the Quieksort example.
  Quicksort.cdl2                 Quicksort example in CDL2 with PowrerShell, C#, and C kernel modules.
  CDL2 Lab.md                    The Lab Usr Guide.
  CDL2-WG.md                     A van Wijngaaredn grammar for CDL2.
  hms.pdf                        An inbtroduction to CDL2 by Kees Koster and Maik Stahl.
  Implementation Notes.md        Some random implementation notes.
  LabCommandsTest.labc           A sample file of Lab commands sutiable for the Lab consult command.        
  md-styles.css                  Used by some MarkDown renderers.

  Windows
    CDL2v1.exe                   The Lab Windows executable. It requires the DLLs listed below to run.
    D3DCompiler_47_cor3.dll
    PenImc_cor3.dll
    PresentationNative_cor3.dll
    vcruntime140_cor3.dll
    wpfgfx_cor3.dll
    cdl2-lab.cmd                 Command to run the Lab on Linux.
    cdl2c.cmd                    Command command to run the Lab as a compiler with a given set of .cdl2 files. It generates a new DB file.

  Linux
    CDL2v1-Linux                 The Lab Linux executable.
    cdl2-lab                     Shell command to run the Lab on Linux.
    cdl2c                        Shell command to run the Lab as a compiler with a given set of .cdl2 files. It generates a new DB file.



NOTES
1. Files with the .md extension are Markdown files. You can open them with a text editor or a Markdown viewer.
   Markdown files are mostly readable as text files, but they can also be rendered with proper formatting.
   On Winddows, you can use Visual Studio Code with a Markdown extension, etc.
   On Linux, you can use a Markdown viewer. One option is
      pandoc file.md -o file.html && xdg-open file.html
   assuming you have already sudo apt install pandoc xdg-utils.
2. The sources are not included in this distribution. They are available on GitHub at
   https://github.com/kovesp/CDL2
   once I make the repo public. The source is licensed under the MIT License, see LICENSE.md for details.
3. The executable files for both platforms are built with Visual Studio 2022 on Windows.
4. When generating code for PowerShell, the generated .ps1 file can be run directly with the pwsh command.
   On Linux you must have installed PowerShell already. The simplest way is
      sudo snap install powershell --classic
   Then use pwsh -v to verify that it worked.
5. When generating C# code, the generated .cs file can be run directly with the dotnet CLI. 
   You need to have the .NET SDK installed on your system. You can download
   it from https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download.
   After installing, you can use the following commands to compile and run the generated C# code: 
      dotnet new console -o MyApp
      mv GeneratedCode.cs MyApp/Program.cs
      cd MyApp
      dotnet run
6. When generating C code, you can compile and run the generated .c file with a C compiler.
   For Windows use the included cc.cmd or cco.cmd to compile. The first command
   generates an executable suitable for debugging, while the second one generates an optimized executable.
   For Linux, just use gcc with appropriate flags (works fine with no flags).
   See CDL2 Lab.md for more details on how to instrument the code for the CDL2 debbuger and profiler.
